{% extends "page/checkout/payment.html.twig" %}

{#
    Sprint 06 — single active payment method.

    With one usable method there is nothing to select, so the payment-selection
    block is replaced by the bare form it lives in: heading, radio buttons,
    prices and descriptions are gone, the <form id="payment"> stays.

    The form has to stay. The step's "next" button sits *outside* it and submits
    it by id (`document.querySelector('#payment').requestSubmit()`), so removing
    the element would leave the customer with no way forward. What remains is
    exactly what the submit needs: the session/navigation parameters, the
    validatepayment action, and the assigned payment id — which core's
    validatePayment() re-validates like any other submission.

    The delivery-set block (`change_shipping`) is a sibling and is decided
    separately — see sprint 07 below. On a shop with one payment method and
    several carriers, this step shows the shipping choice alone.
#}
{% block change_payment %}
    {% if oView.isSinglePaymentAutoAssigned() %}
        <form action="{{ oViewConf.getSslSelfLink()|raw }}" class="payment" id="payment"
              name="order" method="post" novalidate>
            {{ oViewConf.getHiddenSid()|raw }}
            {{ oViewConf.getNavFormParams()|raw }}
            <input type="hidden" name="cl" value="{{ oViewConf.getActiveClassName() }}">
            <input type="hidden" name="fnc" value="validatepayment">
            <input type="hidden" name="paymentid" value="{{ oView.getSinglePaymentId() }}">
        </form>
    {% else %}
        {{ parent() }}
    {% endif %}
{% endblock %}

{#
    Sprint 07 — single active shipping method (revised 2026-08-31).

    Originally the whole `change_shipping` block went, on the reasoning that a
    `<select>` holding one `<option>` offers nothing. That threw away more than
    the control: the customer lost sight of *which* carrier they were getting,
    on the last page where they could still have questioned it.

    So the block stays and only the control goes. The heading, the card and the
    delivery-cost line render as core wrote them; in place of the dropdown the
    carrier's name is printed as text. Shown, not changeable.

    The name comes from the same expression core's own `<option>` uses, off the
    one set `getAllSets()` holds — `isSingleShippingAutoAssigned()` is true only
    when there is exactly one, and this block renders only inside core's
    `{% if oView.getAllSets() %}`, so `|first` always has something to take.

    The surrounding <form> is left alone. It is submitted by the select's own
    `onchange` and by the noscript button, both of which live in this block and
    are now gone — nothing else points at it, so it stands there inert. The
    delivery set itself was written to the session by the controller long
    before this renders.
#}
{% block act_shipping %}
    {% if oView.isSingleShippingAutoAssigned() %}
        <div class="mb-3" id="singleShippingMethod">
            {{ (oView.getAllSets()|first).oxdeliveryset__oxtitle.value }}
        </div>
    {% else %}
        {{ parent() }}
    {% endif %}
{% endblock %}
